# JARVIS Advanced Voice Pipeline Architecture

The JARVIS voice pipeline represents a robust hybrid architecture, utilizing a Node.js central hub to manage long-running Python AI subsystems securely. This documentation provides a visual overview of how audio flows securely and resiliently through your system.

## High-Level Architecture Components

1. **Central Brain (`jarvis.ts`)**: The core Node.js process housing the WebSocket server (`NodeBridge`), memory integrations, LLMs routing (`jarvisBrain`), and CLI fallbacks.
2. **SelfHealingManager (`selfHealingManager.ts`)**: The autonomous supervisor. It spawns the Python scripts securely, intercepts crashes through `FailureDetector`, safely filters standard log outputs, restarts crashed components using exponential backoffs, and maintains circuit breakers to avoid crash loops.
3. **Wake Word Engine (`wakeWords.py`)**: A continuous-listening local daemon. It monitors microphone levels, checks Google STT output for similarity against phrases like `"hey jarvis"`, and triggers the entire pipeline without exhausting local resources.
4. **Speech-To-Text (`stt.py`)**: The dedicated OpenAI Whisper transcriber. It safely loads highly-accurate neural models on a background thread so as to not block the connection queue, records your command strictly upon NodeBridge command, and transcribes exactly what you said.
5. **Text-To-Speech (`tts.py`)**: The responsive Microsoft Edge neural voice output layer. It safely queues outputs sequentially avoiding speech overlaps.

