## 2. Voice Interaction Loop (The Standard Run)

When you are across the room and speak an activation phrase, the system routes everything cleanly.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    actor User as You
    participant Mic as Microphone 
    participant WW as WakeWord (Python)
    participant NB as NodeBridge (TypeScript)
    participant STT as Whisper STT (Python)
    participant Brain as JarvisBrain (LLM)
    participant TTS as EdgeTTS (Python)

    %% Step 1: Trigger
    User->>Mic: "Hey Jarvis..."
    Mic->>WW: Audio Stream
    WW->>WW: Fuzzy recognize "hey jarvis"
    WW->>NB: {"type": "wake_word", "payload": {"detected": true}}
    
    %% Step 2: Listen trigger
    NB->>NB: Filter specific 'stt' role clients
    NB->>STT: {"type": "command", "payload": {"action": "listen"}}
    
    %% Step 3: Command
    STT->>STT: [log] "[STT] Listening..." 
    User->>Mic: "Tell me a joke."
    Mic->>STT: Audio Stream -> Silence detected
    STT->>STT: whisper.transcribe()
    STT->>NB: {"type": "stt_result", "payload": {"text": "Tell me a joke."}}
    
    %% Step 4: AI & Execution
    NB->>Brain: Route string "Tell me a joke."
    Brain->>Brain: Inject memories + System Context
    Brain->>Brain: groq.LLM.chat()
    Brain-->>NB: Respond: "Why did the AI..."
    
    %% Step 5: Speak
    NB->>TTS: {"type": "tts", "payload": {"text": "Why did the AI..."}}
    TTS->>TTS: Generate Neural Voice
    TTS->>User: Play via Speakers
```

> [!NOTE]
> The NodeBridge implements **Role-Aware Routing**. By saving `this.clientRoles.set(ws, role)`, it makes sure that `STT` listen commands don't randomly end up in the Text-To-Speech engine buffer and error out.

