## 3. Self-Healing Crash Mitigation (The Safety Nets)

If any system goes down or locks up, JARVIS automatically kicks off mitigation strategies without crashing to desktop.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant STT as Whisper STT 
    participant FD as FailureDetector
    participant SHM as SelfHealingManager
    participant RE as RepairExecutor
    
    %% Crash Incident
    STT--XSTT: OutOfMemoryError / Traceback Exception
    STT-->>FD: stream stderr
    FD->>FD: regex check isRealError == true
    FD->>SHM: emit("failure", {severity: 'critical', type: 'PythonCrash'})
    
    %% Auto-Healing Logic
    SHM->>SHM: Extract Restart Logic (Attempt 1/3)
    SHM->>RE: repairExecutor.restartProcess("STT")
    RE->>RE: Wait Exponential Backoff (3000ms delay)
    RE->>STT: re-spawn(python, stt.py)
    
    %% Mitigation Feedback
    SHM->>NB: "Sir, I have detecting a failure in my STT module"
```

### Why This Design is "Ideal" For You

1. **Scalability:** Since WS (`ws://127.0.0.1:9000`) acts as the core bus, you could technically run `stt.py` on another fast machine while `tts.py` acts on another, completely distributed.
2. **Resilience:** The Node process (where your LLM tooling / database / API keys live) will practically **never crash** if Python runs out of VRAM or internet acts spotty.
3. **True Autonomy:** The `FailureDetector` doesn't just log out stack traces—it counts failure iterations. After roughly 3 consecutive `Traceback` failures, it completely seals the module with a **Circuit Breaker** to protect desktop stability so you avoid an infinite CPU loop.
